ousands or even millions of compounds  that they could then test against very  specific targeted assays  in high throughput and ultra high  throughput screens  and the whole idea was that if we could  test more targets we would get more  leads  and then we'd be able to generate more  drugs at this stage  safety and toxicology was generally  tested here in the pre-clinical  development stage  so if you look at this kind of drug to  discovery pipeline from the early stage  discovery through to  the the different clinical trials here  just before the clinical trials was  where safety was taken was  actually studied and compounds were  tested for safety  generally this was done in animal  studies now there was a problem with  this that became apparent  whole process became apparent in over  the course of the autism there was  actually  almost a small cottage industry  developed in  publishing papers about this problem is  that  the whole process did not give the  expected return on investment  the number of compounds that were  actually going to market  did not increase in comparison to the  number of programs that were being  generated and the number of compounds  that were going into  clinical trials one of the the major  problems that people discovered was that  a lot of the compounds that were going  into clinical trials were failing  there were a number of reasons for this  efficacy toxicity in animals  but unfortunately also adverse reactions  in humans  in compounds that were getting through  to the market also  pharmac pharmacokinetics the  bioavailability of of compounds  uh some problems with that as as i  mentioned there were  some very high profile market  withdrawals where compounds actually  went through  these trials and then you got problems  in humans  and what was found that in the 25 years  to 1999  about 0.7 approved drugs were withdrawn  per year  but in 1994-2006 this actually went  up to 2.1 approved drugs withdrawn from  the market per year  and 