ofiler is a very  well established  open source image analysis platform and  that is  very suitable for a lot of this work as  regards image management  well again some of the commercial  platforms  have image databases and data management  databases that they  package with their instruments another  option for an open source uh image  analysis database  is the omero platform which is another  like cell profiler it's a very  successful open source  tool as well as the data analytics  for the processing of the numeric data  after you've done your feature  extraction  well when you look at these various  papers and you get excited about doing  that analysis often you'll find  that a lot of the work is done with you  know coding and things like or in python  and naturally you'll need you know  someone who can actually do that to help  you  replicate some of that work but you can  also do  things that are very similar to this  using some other  commercial and open source platforms for  example with our tool strataminer you  can  do phenotypic analysis and build machine  learning models  similarly in spot fire uh which is  marketed by perkin elmer for these uh  types of applications  cell profiler analyst also has some  machine learning and other analytical  tools built in  and then there is a pipelining tool nime  which is  it's kind of between you know coding and  r and tools like spot fire and strata  minor and allows you to build pipelines  using various modules  and do image analysis as regards  direct image classification some of this  type of functionality is built into  some of the image analysis tools such as  practice columbus where you could  identify cells and train models based on  those  also sativa have a tool called phenid  lifts that works with their encarta  these are based on machine learning  of the numeric features there are also  some deep learning tools out there which  we think will be very useful  for for some of this work  at the moment i think 